WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.210
On my way to the grocery store

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.546
mentally preparing myself...

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.298
for the sticker shock.

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:08.091
Here we are.

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:09.092
Wish me luck.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.722
Got my diapers.

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:14.848
It might be something else for you.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.225
For me, it's diapers is really where, like,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.144
I notice inflation the most.

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.438
One of the things that's interesting 
about this particular

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:23.648
period of inflation that 
we're in is

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.692
this is a worldwide phenomenon.

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.069
Hi, Vox. My question is about inflation.

00:00:28.069 --> 00:00:29.821
What is the real cause?

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:32.365
Is it just COVID or we have 
others to blame?

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:33.324
Thank you.

00:00:33.324 --> 00:00:37.662
Diapers in the US, food in Ghana, 
and home prices in India.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:40.665
What actually caused 
all this inflation?

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.918
And is there something 
we can do about it?

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.382
If you watch cable news in the US,

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.801
you will see one explanation 
for inflation

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:53.386
that gets a lot of attention.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:56.681
[overlapping] "Still too much money, 
chasing too few goods."

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:58.767
Too much money chasing 
too few goods.

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:00.935
Say I have a car dealership.

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:02.771
Today, we're in a global pandemic.

00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:04.939
Factories are shutting down periodically

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:08.568
and I'm just not able to keep 
as many cars on my lap.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.695
My inventory is low.

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:13.114
But at the same time, 
I have customers

00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:15.366
willing to spend money on my cars.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.494
My inventory starts to dwindle,

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:20.872
and now there is more customers 
than there are cars.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:23.041
So I can just increase 
the price.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.586
Too much money chasing 
too few goods.

00:01:26.669 --> 00:01:28.546
Economists like this guy, 
Larry Summers

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:30.048
he's been sounding 
the alarm bell

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:32.050
about this kind of inflation 
from the beginning.

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:36.096
"I am much more worried 
that we'll have inflation."

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:37.514
"This is the...

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:41.559
least responsible macroeconomic policies

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:44.938
we've had in the last 40 years."

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:47.816
He is talking about those 
pandemic relief checks.

00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:50.652
Before we can figure out 
if Larry Summers is right

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:51.945
we need to take a closer look at

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:55.031
exactly how inflation is measured 
in the first place.

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:58.243
Once a month, the US Bureau 
of Labor Statistics 

00:01:58.243 --> 00:02:00.954
puts together what it calls 
a market basket.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:03.164
These data are specific to the US

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:05.333
but you can find similar numbers 
around the world.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:09.462
The BLS looks at the prices for 
different goods and services 

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.465
like housing, electricity, 
apparel

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:15.343
prescription drugs, bakery products, 
dairy.

00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:17.303
They look at the price 
we're paying this month

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:20.014
and then compare it to the price
for the same good or service

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:21.641
last month or last year.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:25.145
Then they calculate a single percentage 
that captures all of that change

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:28.148
and that's the Consumer Price Index, 
or CPI.

00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:30.108
And it's true

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.527
the consumer price index 
has been rising steadily

00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:34.237
for the past couple of years.

00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:36.906
Here's when those stimulus checks 
went out.

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.117
But now let's take 
a closer look at 

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:40.869
some of the specific 
goods and services

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:42.287
that make up the CPI.

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:45.790
Some things are way more expensive 
than this time last year

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.586
like fuel oil, airline fares, gas, 
baked goods, dairy products.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.256
But there is some stuff 
that's held fairly steady.

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:55.175
In a normal, healthy economy

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:59.137
the Federal Reserve expects for inflation 
to be about 2% a year.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:02.807
And for stuff like clothing, prescription drugs,
and education

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:05.685
prices are only slightly above that 2% mark.

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.314
for used cars and trucks, gasoline, 
and communication....

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:10.523
That's your phone plan.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:11.774
Internet streaming.

00:03:11.774 --> 00:03:14.444
Prices are actually lower than this time last year.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:17.113
It seems a bit more complicated 
than simply:

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:18.489
People have too much money.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:20.325
If that were the only problem

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:22.285
wouldn't everything be
more expensive?

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:25.914
So unfortunately for me

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:28.124
the Bureau of Labor Statistics
doesn't actually track

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:30.293
the price of diapers 
as part of the CPI.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:34.422
But I did find a private research firm, 
Nielsen IQ, that does.

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:36.674
So, Nielsen looks at something 
called the unit price.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:38.676
Basically, they take the box of diapers

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:41.095
divide it by how many diapers
are in the box

00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:42.555
and that gives you 
the unit price.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:43.932
They get a bunch of different brands

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:45.683
and then come up with this average.

00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:51.022
So in 2019, the average unit price 
for diapers was 16.1 cents.

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:53.983
If prices had gone up at that expected 2%,

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:56.861
annual inflation rate January of 2023

00:03:56.861 --> 00:04:00.281
would have seen me paying 17.4 cents per diaper.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:01.950
But of course, that's not

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.701
what the last few years have looked like.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:04.786
If the price of diapers

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:07.330
had increased at the same rate 
as the CPI...

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:11.876
I would be paying 19 cents per diaper 
in January of 2023.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.378
But no, no, no, no.

00:04:13.378 --> 00:04:17.298
I am currently paying 
21 cents per diaper

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:19.634
an increase of over 30%.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.221
So why is it that the price 
of diapers is rising

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:26.432
so much faster than the price 
of all these other goods?

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:29.435
You can think of the costs of a business
in a couple of ways.

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:32.814
There's the wages that you pay for workers,
their investments 

00:04:32.814 --> 00:04:35.275
in capital and the 
machinery itself.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:37.777
And all of that adds up to 
the cost of production.

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:40.571
And then there's the cost 
you actually sell the good at.

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:42.490
And the difference between 
those two costs

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.533
are called a markup.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:44.993
One of the things that makes it difficult

00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:48.246
to report on inflation is that 
reporters and consumers

00:04:48.246 --> 00:04:50.290
we don't know how much 
it costs companies

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.666
to pay their workers.

00:04:51.666 --> 00:04:53.126
We don't know how much it costs them

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:55.753
to import the materials that they need.

00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:57.338
But we can guess.

00:04:57.338 --> 00:04:58.840
Take labor costs, 
for example.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.592
We know that from 
2019 to 2023...

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:04.762
the average hourly wage for someone 
working in manufacturing...

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:07.015
rose by about 17%.

00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:09.475
Now, that is faster than what

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:12.520
you would expect under normal 
2% annual inflation.

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:15.398
But when you consider the fact that corporations

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:16.941
have successfully kept wages

00:05:16.941 --> 00:05:19.736
so low in the years leading up to that point...

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:22.947
that they were actually declining 
relative to inflation...

00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:25.783
this recent bump starts to look more

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:28.119
like a kind of return to normalcy.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.246
Okay, but what about 
the cost of materials?

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:31.622
I went to the Pampers website 

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:34.208
and looked at what goes into 
making a diaper.

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:36.794
It turns out this stuff 
is made out of wood

00:05:36.794 --> 00:05:39.005
and these are all plastics

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.382
which are made by 
refining petroleum

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:42.800
in slightly different ways

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.636
according to the Federal Reserve's
Producer Price Index...

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:48.556
the average price for 
both of these materials

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:50.808
wood pulp and 
plastic products

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:53.227
has risen significantly over 
the past five years.

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:55.688
But both of these rising input costs...

00:05:55.897 --> 00:06:01.319
still don't completely explain 
the price hike in diapers.

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:05.698
One of the reasons I really wanted
to talk to Rakeen

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.283
is that she and her colleagues 

00:06:07.283 --> 00:06:11.120
have listened to hundreds and hundreds 
of hours of earnings calls.

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:12.080
What we found is

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.665
big corporations who are jacking up prices

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:16.834
beyond what their production cost would justify.

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:19.420
These are some transcripts 
of those earnings calls

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.464
that Rakeen and her colleagues
have been listening to.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.300
The CEO of Hostess Brands, 
for example

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.261
says of these price hikes, 
"Consumers get used to it."

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:29.972
"When all prices go up, 
it helps."

00:06:29.972 --> 00:06:32.392
A Tyson Foods executive claimed that

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:35.645
Price increases for beef "more than offset 
the higher costs."

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:38.689
VISA's CEO says that

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:41.359
historically "inflation has been 
a positive for us."

00:06:41.776 --> 00:06:43.820
All three of these corporations experienced

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.947
record profits over
the past 3 years.

00:06:46.114 --> 00:06:48.408
But what they did with 
those profits is instructive.

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:50.493
They paid that money out 
to their shareholders

00:06:50.493 --> 00:06:52.328
and raised prices 
for the rest of us.

00:06:52.328 --> 00:06:53.788
So we have our three theories

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:55.790
for this current moment of inflation.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.458
Too much money floating around.

00:06:57.458 --> 00:06:58.334
Supply shocks.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:06:59.794
And massive markups.

00:06:59.836 --> 00:07:01.879
Unfortunately, it's probably going to be months

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:03.256
or maybe even years before

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:05.716
we have all of the data necessary 
to figure out

00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:08.678
which one of these 3 explanations 
holds the most weight.

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:11.806
But there are still things we can do

00:07:11.806 --> 00:07:13.057
to help bring prices down.

00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:16.936
And we think of inflation in 
a really singular way...

00:07:17.145 --> 00:07:20.189
and that really limits the 
vast panoply of tools

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:22.483
that we actually have 
to fight this problem.

00:07:22.483 --> 00:07:24.444
Let's start with the tool 
that the Federal Reserve

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:27.029
has already busted out: 
raising interest rates.

00:07:27.822 --> 00:07:29.907
The way that works is 
it makes it more expensive

00:07:29.907 --> 00:07:32.452
for companies to borrow money,
which makes it harder for them

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.829
to invest in new projects 
and hire new people.

00:07:34.871 --> 00:07:37.874
So far, the Fed has raised 
interest rates 8 times

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:40.126
and the unemployment rate 
has stayed pretty low.

00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:42.545
But that could change if 
the Fed keeps raising rates.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:45.590
The last time inflation got super high 
back in the early 80s

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.341
the Fed did huge interest rate hikes

00:07:47.341 --> 00:07:48.676
and inflation did go down.

00:07:48.676 --> 00:07:50.636
But look what happened 
to unemployment.

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:52.263
It shot way up.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:55.308
By 1982, one in 10 people were out of work.

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:57.059
In the last month of 2022...

00:07:57.351 --> 00:07:59.729
prices were starting to come down 
in some key areas.

00:08:00.104 --> 00:08:01.606
Dairy, fruits and veggies

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:03.065
airline fares.

00:08:03.065 --> 00:08:06.360
Prices for these two items, 
gasoline and fuel oil

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.737
are way down.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:10.364
And the reason likely has very little

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:12.575
to do with raising interest rates.

00:08:12.867 --> 00:08:14.160
Over the past 12 months

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.829
President Biden released 
millions of barrels of oil

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:20.041
from the US emergency stockpile, 
which increased global supply

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:21.584
and drove down the price.

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:22.835
There's precedent for doing this

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:24.420
in other areas of the economy.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.923
In 1939, US President Franklin Roosevelt 

00:08:26.923 --> 00:08:29.884
asked Congress for nearly $900 million

00:08:29.884 --> 00:08:32.386
to help the airline industry
quadruple their output.

00:08:32.386 --> 00:08:36.098
"Ships, planes, tanks, guns."

00:08:36.098 --> 00:08:37.725
"That is our purpose."

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.936
"And our pledge."

00:08:41.646 --> 00:08:43.397
Something Rakeen told me 
about diapers

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:44.982
got me thinking about a third way

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:46.901
we might start bringing prices down.

00:08:46.901 --> 00:08:49.987
Turns out that somewhere 
between 70 and 80%

00:08:49.987 --> 00:08:52.114
of all the diapers produced 
in the US

00:08:52.114 --> 00:08:53.991
are made by two companies.

00:08:54.158 --> 00:08:55.618
This honestly threw me a little bit

00:08:55.618 --> 00:08:56.577
but then I went back and looked at

00:08:56.577 --> 00:08:58.746
some of that footage that I shot 
in the grocery store.

00:08:58.746 --> 00:09:00.831
Luvs, Pampers, Ninjamas.

00:09:00.831 --> 00:09:03.125
Those are all made by Procter &amp; Gamble.

00:09:03.251 --> 00:09:05.962
Huggies, Pull-Ups, goodnites, 
all made by Kimberly-Clark.

00:09:06.254 --> 00:09:08.631
So while it looks like parents 
have a lot of choices

00:09:08.631 --> 00:09:09.715
in the diaper aisle

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:11.175
we actually don't.

00:09:11.634 --> 00:09:14.887
Policymakers have allowed 
really rampant deregulation...

00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:18.224
that has facilitated these companies 
having so much power...

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:21.018
in a way that they can jack up prices

00:09:21.018 --> 00:09:22.603
without any cost to them.

00:09:22.812 --> 00:09:25.690
That's supply dial and that 
corporate power dial.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.358
We've let them get dusty.

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:29.610
We do not and we never did

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.487
have to throw workers under the bus

00:09:31.487 --> 00:09:33.281
in order to bring down prices .

